        "旀",
        "靣"
      ],
      "decision": "retain",
      "reason": "The apparently opaque labels are accurate learner-facing gugyeol or reading-mark functions; replacing them with invented concrete objects would teach false semantics."
    },
    {
      "category": "lexical and component-mode gloss splits",
      "decision": "retain",
      "reason": "Side forms and productive components such as 氵, 艹, 扌, 亻, 金, 言, 日, and ⺼ benefit from concrete component-mode labels even when the standalone lexical gloss differs."
    },
    {
      "category": "simplification collisions",
      "decision": "retain distinct concepts",
      "reason": "Pairs such as 廣/广, 幾/几, 兒/儿, 幹/干, 乾/干, 鬥/斗, and the 台 family collapse historically different characters. A single forced gloss would create incorrect mnemonics."
    },
    {
      "category": "stable recurring units and pictographs",
      "decision": "retain selectively",
      "reason": "A component can be worth learning atomically when it recurs as a stable visible chunk, even if it is rare as a standalone word. It is dissolved when its stored lexical meaning is merely archaic or a variant label and the smaller pieces produce a clearer image."
    },
